;; spec:   stdlib §2.5 (a bulk vec builds the trie in one pass), §8.1 item 5 (count cases)
;; expect: accept
;; result: 1056
;; audit:  clean
;; allocs: <= 69
;; roots:  ../../lib support
;; vec-from-array (L9) of 1056 elements: 32 full leaves and a full tail of 32, the largest vector whose
;; leaves fit one root branch of 32, so the builder must stop at that branch and not stack a second level
;; on it. A second level (a root of one branch of 32 leaves, shift 10) reads and grows exactly as the
;; one-level trie does, so only the count tells them apart: it costs 3 more objects (72), and the mutation
;; review (MUT-L9) found the change `(<= n 32)` to `(< n 32)` in the stacking test surviving every case
;; before this one. 69 is measured (the trace's, which includes the array the case
;; makes); the harness's own test lowers the bound by one and requires the failure.
(ns main)

(defun main () -> i64
  (let ((v (vec-from-array (array 1056 0))))
    (vec-count v)))
